  - component: {fileID: 1975264781}
  - component: {fileID: 1975264780}
  m_Layer: 0
  m_Name: Guided Tour
  m_TagString: Untagged
  m_Icon: {fileID: 0}
  m_NavMeshLayer: 0
  m_StaticEditorFlags: 0
  m_IsActive: 1
--- !u!114 &1975264780
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1975264779}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 9cac0f010b2b5aa07b377076e288090a, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CpuTour
  steps:
  - id: cpu_welcome
    title: Welcome to the CPU lab
    text: Welcome to the CPU scheduling lab. A computer has only a few processor
      cores, but many programs that want one. Here there is a single core, and the
      scheduler decides who gets it next.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 887516468a908108d976489e7373b7a9, type: 3}
  - id: cpu_processes
    title: Processes
    text: Each coloured block is a process. It needs the CPU for a number of ticks,
      called its burst time, and it arrives at a certain moment.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: bc302c17a50c34b86a53a0df483e4046, type: 3}
  - id: cpu_queue
    title: The ready queue
    text: Processes that have arrived and want the CPU wait here, in the ready queue.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 49869b509693d60978f806c3cc35b3f7, type: 3}
  - id: cpu_core
    title: The CPU core
    text: The core runs one process at a time. When that process finishes, or the
      scheduler takes the CPU away, the next one moves in.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 9bf96c161c0f0106989e65aefdd85be7, type: 3}
  - id: cpu_desks
    title: Scheduling algorithms
    text: 'Each desk is a scheduling algorithm: first come, first served; shortest
      remaining time first; priority; and round robin. Use a desk to choose one.
      First come, first served is selected now.'
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 7eeebdb25a58aacc8952fe19fc996e34, type: 3}
  - id: cpu_turn
    title: Your turn
    text: 'Your turn. Start the simulation: press RUN on the console, or the RUN
      button.'
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: d0e16c72367d622ddb36eea1b9910437, type: 3}
  - id: cpu_hint
    title: Hint
    text: Press RUN to let the scheduler start handing out the CPU.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 88c2170f4c71a41db8247f1752bf2185, type: 3}
  - id: cpu_gantt
    title: The Gantt chart
    text: 'Watch the timeline on the wall fill in. This is the Gantt chart: which
      process used the CPU, and when.'
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 28beeb7bd77d1af24b2fc87cbe323d0b, type: 3}
  - id: cpu_result
    title: Waiting time
    text: Everyone is done. A process's waiting time is the time it spent ready but
      not running. With first come, first served, short jobs stuck behind a long
      one wait a long time.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 7796801be48a75844be077075069775d, type: 3}
  - id: cpu_outro
    title: Compare them
    text: Now choose another desk, run it again, and compare the waiting times. Shortest
      remaining time first usually waits the least, and round robin is the fairest.
      When you are ready, test yourself at the challenge wall.
    clip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 3c98e712f1e4b00af83a32b6d16b724a, type: 3}
  marker: {fileID: 733497758}
  desktopScreen: {fileID: 1338765935}
  xrScreen: {fileID: 799028399}
  xrMode: {fileID: 133311996}
  desktopCamera: {fileID: 0}
  quiz:
  - question: Which algorithm can take the CPU away when a shorter job arrives?
    options:
    - First come, first served
    - Shortest remaining time first
    - Non-preemptive priority
    correct: 1
    why: 'SRTF is preemptive: it always runs the process with the least work left.'
  - question: "A process's waiting time is\u2026"
    options:
    - its turnaround time minus its burst time
    - its completion time minus its arrival time
    - its burst time minus its arrival time
    correct: 0
    why: Turnaround is the whole stay in the system; take away the time spent running
      and what remains is waiting.
  - question: What does Round Robin give each process?
    options:
    - The CPU until it finishes
    - A share based only on its priority
    - A fixed time slice (quantum), in turn
    correct: 2
    why: 'When the quantum runs out, the process goes to the back of the ready queue:
      fair to everyone.'
  desktopQuiz: {fileID: 1338765933}
  xrQuiz: {fileID: 799028392}
  buttons: {fileID: 1496238867}
  runWaitSeconds: 15
  runSeconds: 45
